yt-dlp is bundled as resources/ytdlp/yt-dlp.exe and run by the app as a child
process. It is a separate program, unmodified, driven over its documented
command line interface; none of its code is linked into this project.

  yt-dlp
    Authors: the yt-dlp contributors, and the youtube-dl project it forked from
    Source: https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp (release 2026.10.21)
    License of the source: The Unlicense (public domain).
    License of the bundled executable: GNU General Public License version 3 or
    later, because the PyInstaller build contains GPLv3+ licensed code. The
    upstream statement is in the project's README under "Licensing". The full
    third-party license text ships next to the binary as
    resources/ytdlp/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt, and the release checksums as
    resources/ytdlp/SHA2-256SUMS.

    sha256 of resources/ytdlp/yt-dlp.exe:
    33121d192d706e62f684a06f80918e3f3a8ba52ac3a65213b6951c39c7d9f555

Because the bundled yt-dlp executable is GPLv3+ and this project is
AGPL-3.0-or-later, the two are compatible and this project stays under the
AGPL. Distributing this repository, or anything built from it, means shipping
resources/ytdlp/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt together with the binary.

The app updates this binary itself: it fetches a release, verifies the
executable against the release's SHA2-256SUMS, and replaces the file. It never
runs yt-dlp's own self-updater, because the upstream release notes warn that
older executables are vulnerable to man-in-the-middle attacks and recommend
installing a new release by hand.

